+{id:'s3',ch:'spot',type:'mc',
+ q:'Spot the bad resolution: a "resolved" PROGRESS.md is committed, and later a reader finds a lone line containing only "=======" mid-file. What happened, and which check should have caught it?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'A conflict marker survived the hand-edit — the resolver deleted the <<<<<<< and >>>>>>> lines but missed the separator; an anchored, length-exact grep for marker lines in the post-merge sweep catches it',c:true, w:'Correct. Partial marker removal is a classic hand-resolution slip; git add happily stages it. The sweep — grep -nE \'^(<{7} |={7}$|>{7} )\' on the files the merge touched — exists precisely for this, anchored and length-exact so Markdown rules and heredocs don\'t false-positive.'},
+  {t:'Git re-inserted the marker during the commit',c:false,w:'Git never writes markers at commit time; markers are written into the WORKTREE at conflict time. Whatever you add is what gets committed — including leftovers.'},
+  {t:'The ======= is a Markdown setext-heading underline and is fine',c:false,w:'Setext underlines sit directly beneath a text line; a stray separator mid-entry between two blank regions is a marker remnant. This ambiguity is exactly why the sweep must be anchored/length-exact AND content-aware.'},
+  {t:'The file needed merge=union to avoid markers entirely',c:false,w:'union avoids markers by silently keeping both sides unordered — trading a visible slip for invisible invariant violations. Worse, not better.'},
+ ]
+},
