+<section>
+<h2>1. Three-Way Merge Mechanics: Base, Ours, Theirs</h2>
+
+<p><code>git merge</code> never compares two files. It compares <strong>three versions</strong> of each file:</p>
+
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>Base</strong> — the file at the <em>merge base</em>, the nearest common ancestor of the two branches. Tonight: <code>git merge-base 24f142b^1 24f142b^2</code> → <code>df5c43c</code>, the last commit both histories share.</li>
+  <li><strong>Ours</strong> — the file on the branch you are standing on (<code>HEAD</code>, the first parent of the merge).</li>
+  <li><strong>Theirs</strong> — the file on the branch being merged in (the second parent).</li>
+</ul>
+
+<p>The base is what makes the merge <em>three-way</em>, and it is the whole trick: by diffing each side against the base, git learns <strong>who changed what</strong>, not just how the two sides differ. The decision rule is applied region by region:</p>
+
+<table>
+  <tr><th>Base</th><th>Ours</th><th>Theirs</th><th>Result</th></tr>
+  <tr><td>A</td><td>A</td><td>A</td><td>A — nobody changed it</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>A</td><td><strong>B</strong></td><td>A</td><td>B — only ours changed it, take ours</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>A</td><td>A</td><td><strong>B</strong></td><td>B — only theirs changed it, take theirs</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>A</td><td><strong>B</strong></td><td><strong>B</strong></td><td>B — both made the identical change</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>A</td><td><strong>B</strong></td><td><strong>C</strong></td><td><span class="del">CONFLICT</span> — both changed the same region differently</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><em>(nothing)</em></td><td><strong>B inserted</strong></td><td><strong>C inserted</strong></td><td><span class="del">CONFLICT</span> — both inserted at the same point; git has no ordering rule</td></tr>
+</table>
+
+<p>Two properties of this algorithm drive everything in this lesson:</p>
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>It is line-based and content-agnostic.</strong> Git does not know a session log from a C file. Its unit of change is the line; its notion of "the same place" is the surrounding unchanged context lines. It has no idea what a "Session number" is, so it cannot know that two different lines both saying <code>### Session 3</code> are a problem — or that two <em>non-colliding</em> edits can add up to one.</li>
+  <li><strong>A conflict is a refusal, not an error.</strong> The bottom two rows are the cases where any automatic choice would be a guess. Git stops, writes both candidates into the file between markers, and makes a human (or tonight, an agent) decide. Everything it <em>can</em> decide from the table above, it decides silently — which is where Section 5's trouble comes from.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<div class="callout">
+  <div class="callout-label">📌 Reading a merge commit</div>
+  A merge commit has two parents: <code>git show 24f142b</code> prints <code>Merge: 19d2688 d63bbbf</code>. When you commit a resolved conflict, git records the conflicted paths in the default message — <code>24f142b</code>'s message carries <code># Conflicts: Silmulator/docs/learning/PROGRESS.md</code>, and <code>f2b6fbf</code>'s carries only <code>index.html</code>. That trailer is tonight's documentary evidence of which collision was loud and which was silent.
+</div>
+</section>
+
+<!-- ============================================================ -->
+<section>
+<h2>2. Why Append-Only Logs Conflict by Construction</h2>
+
+<p><code>PROGRESS.md</code> is an <strong>append-only log</strong>: every overnight session appends one <code>### Session N</code> block at the same structural position — after the last existing entry, immediately before the <code>## Suggested Next Lessons</code> tail. That position is the file's <strong>append anchor</strong>: the pair of stable context lines every writer inserts between.</p>
+
+<pre><code><span class="cm">### Drill 1 (2026-06-20)          ← last shared entry (context above)</span>
+<span class="cm">…entry body…</span>
+                                  <span class="add">← every new session is inserted HERE</span>
+<span class="cm">## Suggested Next Lessons         ← stable tail (context below)</span></code></pre>
+
+<p>Now run the three-way rule from Section 1 over two branches that each logged a session. Both diffs against the base say the same thing: <em>"insert lines between the Drill 1 block and the Suggested heading."</em> Same context above, same context below, different inserted content — that is the bottom row of the table. <strong>Conflict, by construction.</strong></p>
+
+<p>This is the key contrast with source code. Two branches editing <code>msp430.py</code> usually touch different functions, so their hunks have different context and merge cleanly. Two branches appending to a log <em>always</em> touch the same anchor. For a shared append-only file, conflict is not bad luck or bad manners — it is the steady state of parallel work. The same shape applies to <code>CHANGELOG</code>s, migration lists, and this repo's <code>GLOSSARY.md</code> (append-at-end table) and <code>PROGRESS.md</code>.</p>
+
+<div class="callout tip">
+  <div class="callout-label">💡 How projects defuse the anchor</div>
+  Three standard mitigations: <strong>(1) one file per entry</strong> — changelog fragments in a directory (<code>changelog.d/</code>, "towncrier" style), assembled at release time, so parallel entries are parallel <em>files</em> and never collide; <strong>(2) a union merge driver</strong> (<code>.gitattributes</code>: <code>merge=union</code>) that keeps both sides automatically — but see Section 9 for why that is a trap for numbered logs; <strong>(3) accept the conflict and repair the invariants by hand</strong> — this repo's choice, viable because sessions are infrequent and the invariants are easy to assert. Note that choice (1) is exactly the shape of <code>lessons/</code> + the generated <code>index.html</code>: per-entry files plus a derived aggregate.
+</div>
+
+<div class="callout warn">
+  <div class="callout-label">⚠ The anchor moves with the content</div>
+  "Append-only" describes the <em>discipline</em>, not a magic file mode. The moment one branch also <em>renames</em> an old heading (as the 24f142b resolution renamed Session 3 → Session 4), its diff is no longer a pure insertion at the anchor — and that is precisely what lets a later merge slide past the anchor without conflicting. Section 5 is the case study.
+</div>
+</section>
