+Measure](lessons/0009-adc-reference-transfer-functions.html)
+**Concept taught:** an ADC code is a ratio, not a voltage — … (58b7e2d)
+…14 more lines: Covered bullets, Files created, Engagement, Noted gaps…</span>
+<span class="mk">=======</span>                            <span class="cm">← THEIRS: origin/master (via PR #25)</span>
+<span class="theirs">### Session 3 (2026-06-28, overnight)
+**Lesson:** [0008 — Visual Regression Testing](lessons/0008-visual-regression-testing.html)
+**Drill:** [0008 — Visual Regression Testing](drills/0008-visual-regression-testing.html)
+…8 more lines: golden-image bullets, Engagement…</span>
+<span class="mk">&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; origin/master</span>
+<span class="cm">## Suggested Next Lessons          ← stable tail (shared context)</span></code></pre>
+
+<h3>Anatomy of the hunk</h3>
+<ul>
+  <li>Everything between <code>&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt; HEAD</code> and <code>=======</code> is <strong>ours</strong>; everything between <code>=======</code> and <code>&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; origin/master</code> is <strong>theirs</strong>. The labels name the two parents-to-be.</li>
+  <li>With <code>merge.conflictStyle=diff3</code> a third section appears — <code>||||||| merged common ancestors</code> — showing the <strong>base</strong> text. Here it is <strong>empty</strong>: reproducing this merge tonight with <code>git merge-file --diff3</code> shows nothing between <code>|||||||</code> and <code>=======</code>. An empty base section is the fingerprint of <em>both-sides-append</em>: neither side edited anything; both inserted new text at the same anchor.</li>
+  <li>Git wrote the candidates in parent order, ours first. That order is an artifact of which branch you stood on — it is <strong>not</strong> a claim about which entry belongs first in the log.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h3>The committed resolution (24f142b)</h3>
+<p>Both entries record real, disjoint work, so the resolution keeps <strong>both</strong>, applies the log's own ordering rule (chronological), and repairs the numbering invariant that keeping both would break — two "Session 3"s — by renumbering the later entry:</p>
+
+<pre><code><span class="cm">### Drill 1 (2026-06-20) …</span>
+
+<span class="theirs">### Session 3 (2026-06-28, overnight)</span>          <span class="add">← theirs, June: kept, stays Session 3</span>
+<span class="theirs">…visual-testing entry unchanged…</span>
+
+<span class="ours">### Session 4 (2026-07-15, overnight — issue #18)</span> <span class="add">← ours, July: kept, renumbered 3 → 4</span>
+<span class="ours">…ADC entry unchanged apart from the heading…</span>
+
+<span class="cm">## Suggested Next Lessons</span></code></pre>
+
+<p>Note what the resolution had to add that git could not: <em>domain knowledge</em>. Chronological order and unique, increasing session numbers are rules of this file, not rules of git. The merge tool's job ended at presenting the candidates; the resolver's job was to produce a file that satisfies both histories <strong>and</strong> the file's semantics.</p>
+
+<div class="callout">
+  <div class="callout-label">📌 Verify it yourself</div>
+  <code>git show 24f142b</code> — the commit message records the conflicted path. <code>git show 24f142b^1:Silmulator/docs/learning/PROGRESS.md</code> and <code>…^2:…</code> print the two sides; <code>git show $(git merge-base 24f142b^1 24f142b^2):…</code> prints the base. Diff any pair and you can replay the whole story without checking anything out.
+</div>
+</section>
