+<section>
+<h2>7. Merge vs Rebase When SHAs Are Shared</h2>
+
+<p>The alternative at 24f142b would have been <code>git rebase origin/master</code>: replay the feature branch's commits on top of master and get a straight line instead of a join. Rebase has real virtues — but it <strong>rewrites every replayed commit</strong>, giving each a new SHA. For tonight's stack that cost is disqualifying, because the old SHAs are <em>cited in prose</em>:</p>
+
+<ul>
+  <li>PROGRESS.md itself: "Grounded in commit <code>58b7e2d</code>", "<code>a20fbb1</code>", "<code>701853d</code>", "<code>4cb71a1</code>", "<code>07f1f69</code>"…</li>
+  <li>Lesson headers: lesson 0009's label reads "from issue #18, commit 58b7e2d"; its quiz answers reference the commit by SHA.</li>
+  <li>PR bodies and the compliance audit report reference the commits that flipped probes from NON_COMPLIANT to COMPLIANT.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<p>Rebase the branch and every one of those citations dangles: the text still says <code>58b7e2d</code>, but that commit no longer belongs to any branch's history (it survives only until garbage collection), and after the force-push the SHA-links in PR discussions point at orphans. A reader clicking through from the lesson finds nothing. <strong>Merging preserves every existing SHA immutably</strong> and adds one new join commit; the conflict is resolved once, in that commit, and the resolution is itself recorded history (<code>git show 24f142b</code> shows exactly what the resolver chose).</p>
+
+<p>A second, subtler cost: rebasing replays commits <em>one at a time</em>, so a log-file conflict can re-fire at every replayed commit that touched the log, forcing the same resolution repeatedly (<code>git rerere</code> exists to dull that pain). The merge paid the resolution cost exactly once.</p>
+
+<div class="callout">
+  <div class="callout-label">📌 Rule of thumb</div>
+  <strong>Rebase private work; merge shared work.</strong> If a branch has been pushed, reviewed, cited in a PR body, or — as here — cited inside committed documentation, its SHAs are public API. History rewriting breaks whoever holds a reference, and documentation holds references for a very long time.
+</div>
+</section>
+
