+<section>
+<h2>8. Verification: Before, During, and After the Merge</h2>
+
+<h3>Before — predict without touching the worktree</h3>
+<pre><code><span class="cm"># modern (git ≥ 2.38): in-memory merge of two refs; lists conflicted paths</span>
+git merge-tree --write-tree --no-messages HEAD origin/master
+
+<span class="cm"># classic three-argument form: prints the would-be conflict hunks</span>
+git merge-tree $(git merge-base HEAD origin/master) HEAD origin/master
+
+<span class="cm"># where do histories diverge? what has each side done to the file since?</span>
+git merge-base HEAD origin/master
+git log --oneline $(git merge-base HEAD origin/master)..origin/master -- docs/learning/PROGRESS.md</code></pre>
+<p>A <strong>merge-tree dry run</strong> answers "will this conflict, and where?" without moving <code>HEAD</code>, without touching files, without needing a clean tree. For an overnight agent about to converge six branches, this is the difference between planning resolutions and discovering them mid-merge.</p>
+
+<h3>During — see all three versions</h3>
+<pre><code><span class="cm"># show the BASE section inside conflict hunks (one-time or config)</span>
+git config merge.conflictStyle zdiff3     <span class="cm"># or diff3</span>
+
+<span class="cm"># inspect any side of a conflicted file directly from the index</span>
+git show :1:docs/learning/PROGRESS.md     <span class="cm"># stage 1 = base</span>
+git show :2:docs/learning/PROGRESS.md     <span class="cm"># stage 2 = ours</span>
+git show :3:docs/learning/PROGRESS.md     <span class="cm"># stage 3 = theirs</span></code></pre>
+<p>With diff3 style, the empty-base fingerprint of both-sides-append (Section 3) is visible right in the hunk — the fastest possible diagnosis that this is a keep-both situation.</p>
+
+<h3>After — assert, don't assume</h3>
+<pre><code><span class="cm"># 1. no conflict markers survived into the resolution</span>
+grep -rnE <span class="str">'^(&lt;{7} |={7}$|&gt;{7} )'</span> docs/learning/ && echo <span class="str">"MARKERS LEFT"</span>
+
+<span class="cm"># 2. log invariants hold (Section 5's checks)</span>
+grep -oE <span class="str">'^### Session [0-9]+'</span> PROGRESS.md | sort | uniq -d          <span class="cm"># must print nothing</span>
+grep -oE <span class="str">'^### Session [0-9]+'</span> PROGRESS.md | awk <span class="str">'{n=$3+0; if(n&lt;=p) exit 1; p=n}'</span>
+
+<span class="cm"># 3. derived files regenerated, not inherited from either parent</span>
+python3 scripts/build_learning_index.py && git diff --stat -- docs/learning/index.html
+
+<span class="cm"># 4. read the merge itself</span>
+git show --stat HEAD          <span class="cm"># did the merge bring what you expected?</span>
+git log --merges --oneline -5 <span class="cm"># which joins happened tonight?</span></code></pre>
+
+<div class="callout tip">
+  <div class="callout-label">💡 Marker sweep subtlety</div>
+  Grep for markers <em>anchored and length-exact</em> (<code>^&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt; </code>, seven characters then a space) — a plain <code>grep '&lt;&lt;&lt;'</code> false-positives on heredocs and C++ shifts, and a plain <code>grep '==='</code> hits Markdown horizontal rules. And note this very lesson file <em>displays</em> marker-shaped text: content-aware exclusions (or checking only files the merge touched) keep the sweep honest.
+</div>
+</section>
