+<section>
+<h2>9. Often Confused</h2>
+
+<table>
+  <tr><th>Misconception</th><th>The reality</th></tr>
+  <tr>
+    <td><strong>"Git understands the content it merges"</strong></td>
+    <td>Git merges <em>lines</em> using context to locate regions. It has no notion of session numbers, chronology, or Markdown structure. Every semantic rule of the file is the resolver's job — before, during, and after the merge.</td>
+  </tr>
+  <tr>
+    <td><strong>"No conflict means the merge is correct"</strong></td>
+    <td>No conflict means git had no <em>questions</em>. f2b6fbf's PROGRESS.md auto-merge exited 0 and produced a duplicate Session 4 with an off-by-one tail. Textual success is necessary, not sufficient — invariants must be re-checked after every merge of a shared file.</td>
+  </tr>
+  <tr>
+    <td><strong>"A conflict means somebody made a mistake"</strong></td>
+    <td>For append-only logs, conflict is the designed outcome of correct parallel work: every writer inserts at the same anchor, so both-sides-append <em>must</em> conflict. The mistake would be resolving it by deletion.</td>
+  </tr>
+  <tr>
+    <td><strong>"<code>--ours</code> / <code>--theirs</code> is a resolution"</strong></td>
+    <td>It is a <em>choice of loss</em>. Valid when the sides are two versions of the same record (keep the revision); destructive when they are records of different events — it silently erases one branch's history.</td>
+  </tr>
+  <tr>
+    <td><strong>"<code>merge=union</code> fixes log files"</strong></td>
+    <td>The union driver keeps both sides automatically — with no ordering guarantee, no deduplication, and no renumbering. It would have committed the duplicate-Session-4 file <em>without even the chance</em> to notice. It converts loud conflicts into silent invariant violations: the exact failure mode of Section 5, everywhere, forever.</td>
+  </tr>
+  <tr>
+    <td><strong>"Rebase is just a cleaner merge"</strong></td>
+    <td>Rebase rewrites SHAs. When commits are cited — PR bodies, audit reports, PROGRESS.md's "grounded in 58b7e2d" — rewriting orphans every citation. Merge preserves cited history and records the resolution once, in the join commit.</td>
+  </tr>
+  <tr>
+    <td><strong>"In an add/add conflict, take the newer file"</strong></td>
+    <td>If the file is derived (both index.html candidates were), <em>neither</em> side is right: regenerate from the merged sources. "Newer" is a fact about timestamps, not correctness.</td>
+  </tr>
+  <tr>
+    <td><strong>Conflict <em>hunk</em> vs conflict <em>markers</em></strong></td>
+    <td>The hunk is the disputed region with both candidates; the markers (<code>&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;</code> <code>=======</code> <code>&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;</code>) are the delimiters git writes around it. Resolving means replacing the whole hunk with final text; leaving any marker behind is a corruption the post-merge sweep exists to catch.</td>
+  </tr>
+</table>
+</section>
