+// ─── CHANNEL: mech ────────────────────────────────────────────
+{id:'m1',ch:'mech',type:'w',
+ q:'Name the three inputs of a three-way merge and where each came from in tonight\'s 24f142b merge. Then state the decision rule git applies per region — including the two cases that produce a conflict.',
+ c:[{l:'base = merge-base (df5c43c, nearest common ancestor)',t:['merge-base','merge base','common ancestor','df5c43c']},
+    {l:'ours = HEAD / first parent (feature/issue-18)',t:['ours','head','first parent','feature branch','issue-18']},
+    {l:'theirs = branch merged in / second parent (origin/master)',t:['theirs','second parent','origin/master','master']},
+    {l:'one side changed → take it; both same → take; both differ same region → conflict',t:['only one','take that side','both changed','same region','conflict']},
+    {l:'both inserted at the same point (empty base) → conflict too',t:['insert','same point','same anchor','empty base','no ordering']}],
+ m:`Base: the file at the merge base — the nearest common ancestor of the two branches (git merge-base 24f142b^1 24f142b^2 = df5c43c). Ours: the file on HEAD, the branch being stood on (feature/issue-18-adc-reference-scaling, the first parent). Theirs: the file on the branch merged in (origin/master, the second parent).
+Decision rule, applied region by region using diffs of each side against the base:
+- neither side changed a region → keep it;
+- exactly one side changed it → take that side's version (this is why the base matters: it reveals WHO changed what, not just that the sides differ);
+- both sides made the identical change → take it once;
+- both sides changed the same region differently → CONFLICT;
+- both sides inserted different text at the same point (nothing at base — the append case) → CONFLICT, because git has no ordering rule for two insertions.
+Git stops only for the last two; everything else is decided silently.`
+},
