+{id:'m2',ch:'mech',type:'mc',
+ q:'Why does an append-only log like PROGRESS.md conflict "by construction" when two branches each log a session?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'Every writer inserts at the same append anchor — between the last shared entry and the stable tail heading — so both diffs against the base describe different insertions at the same point',c:true, w:'Correct. Same context above (end of the Drill 1 block), same context below (## Suggested Next Lessons), different inserted text: the exact both-inserted-at-one-point case git must refuse. Unlike source code, where parallel edits usually land in different functions, log appends always collide.'},
+  {t:'Markdown headings confuse the merge algorithm\'s context matching',c:false,w:'Git does not parse Markdown at all — headings are just lines. The collision comes from both insertions sharing the same surrounding context, which would be true in any format.'},
+  {t:'Log files are usually large, and large files always conflict',c:false,w:'Size is irrelevant; f2b6fbf auto-merged five inserted session blocks cleanly. What matters is whether the changed regions overlap.'},
+  {t:'Git locks append-only files so only one branch may modify them',c:false,w:'Git has no file locking and no append-only mode — "append-only" describes the team\'s discipline for the file, not a git feature.'},
