+{id:'m4',ch:'mech',type:'mc',
+ q:'Branch A renames a heading at line 47 of a file. Branch B appends five blocks after line 65 of the same file. What happens when they merge?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'Clean auto-merge — the changed regions do not overlap, so git takes both changes without asking anyone',c:true, w:'Correct, and it is exactly the f2b6fbf setup: theirs renamed "Session 3" → "Session 4" (plus inserted the June entry) around line 47; ours appended Sessions 4–8 after line 65. Git combined them silently — producing a duplicate "Session 4". Clean is not the same as correct.'},
+  {t:'A conflict, because both branches modified the same file',c:false,w:'Conflicts are per-REGION, not per-file. Same file + disjoint regions = clean merge; that is the everyday case that makes git usable at all.'},
+  {t:'Git refuses to merge because one change is a rename',c:false,w:'Renaming a LINE\'s content is just an edit; git has no special handling for it. (File renames are detected separately and also merge fine.)'},
+  {t:'Branch B\'s appends are discarded because Branch A\'s change is earlier in the file',c:false,w:'Position in the file grants no priority — both non-conflicting changes are kept, which is precisely how the semantic breakage combined.'},
