+// ─── CHANNEL: diag ────────────────────────────────────────────
+{id:'d1',ch:'diag',type:'w',
+ q:'State the diagnostic question for a log-file conflict hunk, and list the evidence signals that point to keep-both versus keep-one versus regenerate.',
+ c:[{l:'ask: two records of different events, or two versions of one record?',t:['different events','same record','two versions','one record','question']},
+    {l:'keep-both: different dates / issue numbers / artifacts referenced',t:['different date','issue number','disjoint','artifact','keep both']},
+    {l:'keep-one: revision (typo fix, extra bullet) or verbatim superset',t:['revision','superset','typo','keep one','better version']},
+    {l:'regenerate: file is derived/generated output',t:['derived','generated','regenerate','lockfile','index']}],
+ m:`The question is never "ours or theirs?" — it is: are the two sides two records of DIFFERENT events, or two versions of the SAME record?
+- Keep both (ordered, renumbered): the sides cite different dates, different issue numbers, and reference different artifacts. 24f142b: 2026-06-28/lesson 0008 vs 2026-07-15/lesson 0009 — disjoint work, both true. Deleting either falsifies history, especially since the merge itself brought the other side's lesson files into the tree.
+- Keep one: same event, one side revised it (typo fixes, an added bullet, corrected link) — keep the revision; or one side's text contains the other's verbatim plus more — keep the superset.
+- Keep neither / regenerate: the file is derived output (index.html, lockfiles, generated reports). Neither side is the source of truth; resolve the sources and rerun the generator.
+Order for kept entries comes from the log's own invariant (chronological in PROGRESS.md), never from git's ours-first hunk layout.`
+},
+
+{id:'d2',ch:'diag',type:'mc',
+ q:'In the 24f142b conflict, which single fact makes keep-both not merely polite but REQUIRED for consistency?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'The very same merge brings lessons/0008-visual-regression-testing.html into the tree — dropping master\'s log entry would leave the merged tree containing a lesson the log claims was never taught',c:true, w:'Correct. The log and the tree must agree. Master\'s Session 3 documents artifacts that arrive in this exact merge commit (the 0008 lesson, drill, goldens); a log that omits the entry contradicts the tree it sits in.'},
+  {t:'Git rejects resolutions that delete either side of a conflict hunk',c:false,w:'Git accepts ANY resolution text, including taking one side or deleting both. Correctness is entirely on the resolver — that is the point of the lesson.'},
+  {t:'The feature branch\'s entry was newer, and newer entries always win',c:false,w:'Recency decides nothing; and if it did, "winning" would mean deleting the June record — precisely the data loss keep-both avoids.'},
+  {t:'Both entries had the same session number, so both had to be kept to preserve the number',c:false,w:'The shared "Session 3" number is the INVARIANT VIOLATION keep-both must repair (by renumbering), not a reason for keeping both.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'d3',ch:'diag',type:'mc',
+ q:'Having decided to keep both Session-3 entries, what determines their order in the resolved file?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'The log\'s own ordering invariant — chronological — so the June visual-testing entry precedes the July ADC entry, even though "ours" appeared first in the hunk',c:true, w:'Correct. Git prints ours above theirs purely because of which branch you stood on; it is presentation, not semantics. PROGRESS.md is chronological, so 2026-06-28 goes before 2026-07-15, and the July entry takes the next number (Session 4).'},
+  {t:'Hunk order — git lists ours first, so ours stays first',c:false,w:'Hunk order is an artifact of parent order. Standing on master and merging the feature branch would have flipped it while the correct resolution stayed the same.'},
+  {t:'Alphabetical order of the lesson titles',c:false,w:'Nothing in the file sorts alphabetically; its rule is chronological. A differently-ruled log (severity, alphabetical) would use ITS rule — the point is the file\'s rule outranks the tool\'s layout.'},
+  {t:'The larger entry goes first so context is established early',c:false,w:'Entry size is meaningless to ordering. Only the log\'s own invariant counts.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'d4',ch:'diag',type:'mc',
+ q:'A different conflict: ours has "fixed typo, added one bullet" on Session 2\'s entry; theirs has Session 2\'s entry untouched plus a brand-new Session 3 below it. Diagnosis?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'No dilemma at all — these regions don\'t truly compete: keep ours\' revised Session 2 AND theirs\' new Session 3; it is a revision plus a new record, not two versions of one thing',c:true, w:'Correct. Apply the diagnosis per REGION: the Session-2 edits are a same-record revision (keep the revised one); the Session-3 block is a new event (keep it). If git presented them as one hunk, the resolution interleaves both — resolution is per-record, not per-hunk.'},
+  {t:'Keep-one: theirs, because it contains more sessions',c:false,w:'That discards ours\' typo fix and bullet. "More content" is not the superset test — theirs does NOT contain ours\' revision verbatim.'},
+  {t:'Keep-one: ours, because revisions outrank additions',c:false,w:'That deletes theirs\' entire new session. There is no rank between revisions and additions; both survive because they concern different records.'},
+  {t:'Regenerate the file from scratch',c:false,w:'PROGRESS.md is a source-of-truth log, not derived output — there is no generator to rerun. Regeneration applies to files like index.html.'},
+ ]
