+// ─── CHANNEL: repair ──────────────────────────────────────────
+{id:'r1',ch:'repair',type:'w',
+ q:'List PROGRESS.md\'s semantic invariants, and state which of them the f2b6fbf auto-merge violated while git reported success.',
+ c:[{l:'unique session numbers',t:['unique','duplicate','once']},
+    {l:'monotonically increasing top to bottom',t:['monoton','increasing','ascending','order of numbers']},
+    {l:'chronological entry order',t:['chronolog','date order','by date']},
+    {l:'single Suggested Next Lessons tail after the last entry',t:['suggested next','tail','one heading','single']},
+    {l:'violated: uniqueness (two Session 4s) and monotonicity (tail off by one)',t:['violated','two session 4','duplicate session 4','off by one','off-by-one']}],
+ m:`Invariants:
+1. Session numbers are unique — each ### Session N heading appears once;
+2. numbers are monotonically increasing top to bottom;
+3. entries are in chronological order;
+4. exactly one "## Suggested Next Lessons" tail follows the last entry.
+The f2b6fbf auto-merge (exit 0, zero conflict markers) violated 1 and 2: theirs' rename made issue #18 "Session 4" while ours appended its own "Session 4" (issue #19), giving a duplicate heading, and ours' tail (issues #20–#23 as Sessions 5–8) was off by one relative to the renumbered sequence. Invariants 3 and 4 happened to survive. Git checks none of these — they are file semantics, invisible to a line-based merge; that is why they must be asserted after every merge, especially the clean ones.`
+},
+
+{id:'r2',ch:'repair',type:'mc',
+ q:'What was the actual repair applied to the f2b6fbf auto-merge result before committing?',
+ snip:`<span class="cm"># auto-merged headings (broken)         # committed f2b6fbf (repaired)</span>
+### Session 4 (… — issue #18)      →   ### Session 4 (… — issue #18)
+<span class="mk">### Session 4 (… — issue #19)</span>      →   ### Session 5 (… — issue #19)
+### Session 5 (… — issue #20)      →   ### Session 6 (… — issue #20)
+### Session 6 (… — issue #21)      →   ### Session 7 (… — issue #21)
+### Session 7 (… — issue #22)      →   ### Session 8 (… — issue #22)
+### Session 8 (… — issue #23)      →   ### Session 9 (… — issue #23)`,
+ opts:[
+  {t:'Renumber the five appended headings (#19–#23 become Sessions 5–9), leave every entry body untouched, then assert uniqueness before committing',c:true, w:'Correct. The June entry stays Session 3 and issue #18 stays Session 4 (the 24f142b renumbering is honoured); only the five stale numbers move up by one. The content of every entry is already right — the repair touches exactly the invariant-carrying lines, then proves it with the uniq -d check.'},
+  {t:'Delete the second "Session 4" entry since duplicates are forbidden',c:false,w:'The duplicate NUMBER is the bug, not the entry — issue #19\'s session really happened. Deleting it erases history; renumbering repairs the invariant losslessly.'},
+  {t:'Re-run the merge with -X ours to prefer the stack\'s numbering',c:false,w:'-X ours only biases CONFLICTED hunks; this file had none, so the strategy option changes nothing. And preferring ours wholesale would drop theirs\' June entry and renumbering.'},
+  {t:'Renumber the June entry to Session 9 so it slots after the stack\'s sessions',c:false,w:'That breaks chronological order (invariant 3) to rescue numbering — moving a June session after five July ones. The tail renumber fixes uniqueness AND keeps chronology.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'r3',ch:'repair',type:'w',
+ q:'Write the two shell checks that assert PROGRESS.md\'s numbering invariants (uniqueness and monotonicity), and explain what each outputs on the broken auto-merge file.',
+ c:[{l:'grep -oE ^### Session [0-9]+ | sort | uniq -d for duplicates',t:['uniq -d','sort','grep -o']},
+    {l:'empty output = pass; broken file prints "### Session 4"',t:['empty','prints','session 4','nothing']},
+    {l:'awk n<=p exit 1 for monotonic increase',t:['awk','exit 1','n<=p','monoton','p=n']},
+    {l:'run them post-merge / pre-commit because clean merges are silent',t:['post-merge','pre-commit','clean merge','silent','ci']}],
+ m:`# uniqueness — any duplicated heading is printed; empty output = invariant holds
+grep -oE '^### Session [0-9]+' PROGRESS.md | sort | uniq -d
+# monotonicity — exits 1 at the first number that fails to increase
+grep -oE '^### Session [0-9]+' PROGRESS.md | awk '{n=$3+0; if(n<=p) exit 1; p=n}'
+On the broken f2b6fbf auto-merge file the first check prints "### Session 4" (it appears twice: issues #18 and #19), and the second exits 1 when the second Session 4 fails to exceed the first. On the repaired committed file both pass: no output, exit 0.
+Notes: the -o flag extracts just the matching heading text so sort|uniq -d compares clean keys; the pattern anchors on '^### Session ' so "### Drill 1" is ignored; $3 is the number field, +0 forces numeric comparison. Because the failure mode is a merge git calls SUCCESSFUL, these belong in a pre-commit hook or CI, not in a human's memory.`
+},
+
+{id:'r4',ch:'repair',type:'mc',
+ q:'Where must the PROGRESS.md invariant guard run to catch the f2b6fbf failure class, and why exactly there?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'After every merge, before the result is committed/pushed (pre-commit hook or CI) — because the failure arrives via merges git reports as SUCCESSFUL, so "when git complains" never fires',c:true, w:'Correct. A conflict is its own alarm; the dangerous case is the clean auto-merge, which produces no signal at all. The only reliable interception point is a check that runs unconditionally on the result — the same philosophy as the audit probes: assert behaviour, don\'t trust silence.'},
+  {t:'Only when git reports a conflict in the file',c:false,w:'That is precisely the case that DOESN\'T need it — a conflict already forces a human through the file. f2b6fbf\'s PROGRESS.md never conflicted and was still broken.'},
+  {t:'In the merge driver itself, replacing git\'s text merge',c:false,w:'A custom merge driver is heavy machinery and still has to fall back to text merging; validating the RESULT is simpler, tool-agnostic, and also catches hand-editing mistakes.'},
+  {t:'Nightly, on a schedule',c:false,w:'A nightly check finds the breakage after it may have been pushed, cited, and built upon. The guard must gate the commit, not audit it later.'},
+ ]
+},
