+{id:'p5',ch:'repo',type:'w',
+ q:'Argue the merge-vs-rebase decision for tonight\'s stack: what concretely breaks if feature/issue-18 is rebased onto master instead of merged, and what does the merge cost in exchange?',
+ c:[{l:'rebase rewrites replayed commits → new SHAs',t:['rewrit','new sha','rewrites','different sha','force-push','force push']},
+    {l:'SHAs are cited in prose: PROGRESS.md "grounded in 58b7e2d", lesson headers, PR bodies, audit report',t:['cited','58b7e2d','pr bod','audit','progress.md','prose','documentation']},
+    {l:'citations dangle/orphan after rewrite; PR links break',t:['dangl','orphan','broken link','no longer','gc','garbage']},
+    {l:'rebase can replay the log conflict per commit (rerere)',t:['per commit','replay','each commit','rerere','repeatedly']},
+    {l:'merge cost: non-linear history + one join commit',t:['non-linear','nonlinear','join commit','merge commit','history graph','messier']}],
+ m:`Rebasing replays each feature-branch commit onto master, creating NEW commits with new SHAs. But this stack's SHAs are cited in committed prose: PROGRESS.md says "Grounded in commit 58b7e2d" (and a20fbb1, 701853d, 4cb71a1, 07f1f69…), lesson 0009's header and quiz cite 58b7e2d, and PR bodies and the compliance audit report cite the commits that flipped probes to COMPLIANT. After a rebase + force-push, every one of those citations points at an orphaned object — resolvable locally until GC, linked to nothing on the remote. A reader clicking through from the docs finds a dead SHA.
+Secondary cost: rebase replays commits one at a time, so the PROGRESS.md append conflict can re-fire at each replayed commit that touched the log (git rerere mitigates, but the merge paid the resolution exactly once, recorded in 24f142b).
