+<section>
+<h2>4. Keep-One vs Keep-Both: Diagnosing a Log Conflict</h2>
+
+<p>Faced with a conflict hunk in a log, the question is never "ours or theirs?" — it is: <strong>are these two records of <em>different events</em>, or two versions of the <em>same record</em>?</strong> The hunk itself usually contains the evidence:</p>
+
+<table>
+  <tr><th>Evidence in the hunk</th><th>Diagnosis</th><th>Resolution</th></tr>
+  <tr>
+    <td>Different dates, different issue numbers, different artifacts referenced (2026-06-28/lesson 0008 vs 2026-07-15/lesson 0009)</td>
+    <td>Two different events — disjoint parallel work</td>
+    <td><strong>Keep both</strong>, ordered by the log's own rule; repair numbering</td>
+  </tr>
+  <tr>
+    <td>Same event, but one side has typo fixes, an extra bullet, a corrected link</td>
+    <td>A revision of one record</td>
+    <td><strong>Keep one</strong> — the revised version</td>
+  </tr>
+  <tr>
+    <td>One side's text contains the other side's text verbatim plus more</td>
+    <td>A superset — one side kept writing</td>
+    <td><strong>Keep one</strong> — the superset</td>
+  </tr>
+  <tr>
+    <td>The file is generated output (an index, a lockfile, a report)</td>
+    <td>Neither side is the source of truth</td>
+    <td><strong>Keep neither</strong> — regenerate from the merged sources (Section 6)</td>
+  </tr>
+</table>
+
+<p>Applied to 24f142b: the dates differ by seventeen days, the issue numbers differ, and each entry documents files the <em>other branch does not have</em>. Crucially, the merge itself brought master's artifacts into the tree — <code>lessons/0008-visual-regression-testing.html</code> arrived in the very same merge commit. Deleting master's Session 3 entry would leave the merged tree containing a lesson that the learning log claims was never taught. <strong>Keep-both is not generosity; it is the only resolution consistent with the merged tree.</strong></p>
+
+<h3>Ordering the kept entries</h3>
+<p>The order comes from the log's invariant, not from git's ours-first presentation. PROGRESS.md is chronological, so the June visual-testing entry precedes the July ADC entry — even though "ours" was the July entry and appeared first in the hunk. A log ordered by severity, or alphabetically, would demand its own order. <em>The file's rules outrank the tool's layout.</em></p>
+
+<div class="callout warn">
+  <div class="callout-label">⚠ The --ours reflex</div>
+  <code>git checkout --ours PROGRESS.md</code> (or <code>--theirs</code>) resolves the conflict in one keystroke by <strong>silently deleting the other branch's history</strong>. For source files with a true either/or conflict that can occasionally be right. For an append-only log it is almost always wrong: both-sides-append means both sides are true. The one-keystroke resolution turns a loud conflict into exactly the kind of silent data loss the conflict existed to prevent.
+</div>
+</section>
+
+<!-- ============================================================ -->
+<section>
+<h2>5. The Silent Bad Merge — f2b6fbf, Where "No Conflict" Lied</h2>
+
+<p>Two merges later the stack converged: <code>f2b6fbf</code> merged <code>origin/feature/issue-19-timer-compliance</code> (at <code>a122eb4</code>, which carried master's June Session 3 <em>plus</em> the 24f142b renumbering of the ADC entry to Session 4) into the stack tip <code>903605b</code> (which still had the ADC entry as Session 3, followed by its own appended Sessions 4–8 for issues #19–#23). This time git reported <strong>no conflict in PROGRESS.md at all</strong> — the commit message lists only <code>index.html</code>.</p>
+
+<h3>Why the anchor did not fire</h3>
+<p>Diff each side against the new merge base (<code>701853d</code>, whose log ends at the ADC entry as Session 3):</p>
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>Theirs</strong> changed the region <em>around line 47</em>: inserted the June Session 3 block above the ADC entry, and renamed the ADC heading <code>### Session 3 (…issue #18)</code> → <code>### Session 4 (…issue #18)</code>.</li>
+  <li><strong>Ours</strong> inserted <em>after line 65</em> — five new session blocks appended below the end of the ADC entry's body.</li>
+</ul>
+<p>Different regions, non-overlapping context. Per Section 1's table, git takes both changes — silently. Reproducing it tonight with <code>git merge-file</code>: <strong>exit code 0, zero conflict markers</strong>, and this file:</p>
+
+<pre><code>### Session 3 (2026-06-28, overnight)              <span class="cm">← from theirs</span>
+<span class="theirs">### Session 4 (2026-07-15, overnight — issue #18)</span>  <span class="cm">← theirs' rename of the ADC entry</span>
+<span class="ours">### Session 4 (2026-07-15, overnight — issue #19)</span>  <span class="del">← ours' append — DUPLICATE heading</span>
+<span class="ours">### Session 5 (2026-07-15, overnight — issue #20)</span>  <span class="del">← off by one (should be 6)</span>
+<span class="ours">### Session 6 (2026-07-15, overnight — issue #21)</span>  <span class="del">← off by one (should be 7)</span>
+<span class="ours">### Session 7 (2026-07-15, overnight — issue #22)</span>  <span class="del">← off by one (should be 8)</span>
+<span class="ours">### Session 8 (2026-07-15, overnight — issue #23)</span>  <span class="del">← off by one (should be 9)</span></code></pre>
+
+<p>Every individual line-level decision was correct. The <em>sum</em> is broken: ours numbered its appends 4–8 on the assumption that the ADC entry was Session 3; theirs invalidated that assumption by renumbering it. Git merged the <strong>edits</strong> faithfully and merged the <strong>assumption</strong> not at all — it cannot see assumptions.</p>
+
+<h3>Textual success ≠ semantic success</h3>
+<p>PROGRESS.md has invariants no line-based tool knows about:</p>
+<ol>
+  <li>Session numbers are <strong>unique</strong>;</li>
+  <li>they are <strong>monotonically increasing</strong> top to bottom;</li>
+  <li>entries are in <strong>chronological order</strong>;</li>
+  <li>exactly one <code>## Suggested Next Lessons</code> tail follows the last entry.</li>
+</ol>
+<p>The auto-merge satisfied git completely and violated invariants 1 and 2. This is the central lesson: <strong>git guarantees only that the result is line-consistent with both parents.</strong> A conflict is loud; a semantically bad clean merge is silent. The scarier failure mode is the one that produces no message.</p>
+
+<h3>Post-merge invariant repair</h3>
+<p>The fix committed in <code>f2b6fbf</code>: renumber the five appended headings (#19: 4→5, #20: 5→6, #21: 6→7, #22: 7→8, #23: 8→9), then <em>assert</em> the invariants before committing rather than eyeballing them:</p>
+
+<pre><code><span class="cm"># uniqueness: duplicated session headings print here — output must be empty</span>
+grep -oE <span class="str">'^### Session [0-9]+'</span> PROGRESS.md | sort | uniq -d
+
+<span class="cm"># monotonicity: exit 1 if any session number fails to increase</span>
+grep -oE <span class="str">'^### Session [0-9]+'</span> PROGRESS.md \
+  | awk <span class="str">'{ n = $3 + 0; if (n &lt;= p) exit 1; p = n }'</span></code></pre>
+
+<p>Both checks pass on the committed tree: <code>git show f2b6fbf:…/PROGRESS.md</code> has Sessions 1–9, each number once. The repair took one minute; <em>finding out you need one</em> is the skill — which is why the checks belong in a post-merge routine, not in your memory.</p>
+
+<div class="callout warn">
+  <div class="callout-label">⚠ The general rule</div>
+  After any merge touching a shared log, <strong>re-read the merged file even when git reports no conflicts</strong> — or better, run its invariant checks. "No conflict markers" tells you git had no questions. It does not tell you the answers were right.
+</div>
+</section>
+
+<!-- ============================================================ -->
+<section>
+<h2>6. Derived Files: Regenerate, Don't Merge</h2>
+
+<p>The conflict f2b6fbf <em>did</em> report was <code>docs/learning/index.html</code> — and it was an <strong>add/add conflict</strong>: the file was absent at the merge base <code>701853d</code> and had been created independently on both sides (a 43-line hand-rolled table on one, a 54-line generated page on the other; they even disagree on <code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;</code> vs <code>&lt;!doctype html&gt;</code>). With no base version at all, git can only offer the two whole files.</p>
+
+<p>Hunk-by-hunk merging two independently generated HTML files is answering the wrong question. <code>index.html</code> is not a source of truth — it is a <strong>derived file</strong>, a projection of the <code>lessons/</code> and <code>drills/</code> directories, and the repo carries its generator: <code>scripts/build_learning_index.py</code>. The committed resolution took <em>neither</em> side:</p>
+
+<pre><code><span class="cm"># resolve the SOURCES (lesson/drill files merged fine), then rebuild the projection</span>
+python3 scripts/build_learning_index.py
+git add docs/learning/index.html</code></pre>
+
+<p>The result (44 lines) matches neither parent byte-for-byte and is more correct than both: it indexes the <em>union</em> of lessons that the merged tree actually contains.</p>
+
+<table>
+  <tr><th>File kind</th><th>Merge strategy</th></tr>
+  <tr><td>Source of truth (lesson HTML, Python code, PROGRESS.md entries)</td><td>Merge; resolve conflicts by diagnosis (Section 4)</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Derived artifact (index.html, lockfiles, generated reports, build output)</td><td>Never hand-merge — resolve the sources, then <strong>regenerate</strong></td></tr>
+</table>
+
+<div class="callout tip">
+  <div class="callout-label">💡 Recognizing a derived file under conflict</div>
+  Ask: "if I deleted this file, could the repo recreate it with one command?" If yes, that command <em>is</em> the resolution. Fighting through its hunks by hand can only reproduce what the generator does — at best. This is also why tonight's session does not touch <code>index.html</code>: it is rebuilt separately after the new lesson files land.
+</div>
+</section>
+
+<!-- ============================================================ -->
+<section>
+<h2>7. Merge vs Rebase When SHAs Are Shared</h2>
+
+<p>The alternative at 24f142b would have been <code>git rebase origin/master</code>: replay the feature branch's commits on top of master and get a straight line instead of a join. Rebase has real virtues — but it <strong>rewrites every replayed commit</strong>, giving each a new SHA. For tonight's stack that cost is disqualifying, because the old SHAs are <em>cited in prose</em>:</p>
+
+<ul>
+  <li>PROGRESS.md itself: "Grounded in commit <code>58b7e2d</code>", "<code>a20fbb1</code>", "<code>701853d</code>", "<code>4cb71a1</code>", "<code>07f1f69</code>"…</li>
+  <li>Lesson headers: lesson 0009's label reads "from issue #18, commit 58b7e2d"; its quiz answers reference the commit by SHA.</li>
+  <li>PR bodies and the compliance audit report reference the commits that flipped probes from NON_COMPLIANT to COMPLIANT.</li>
+</ul>
+
