+<div class="term" id="three-way-merge">
+  <h2>Three-way merge</h2>
+  <div class="pron">base + ours + theirs → result</div>
+  <dl>
+    <dt>Definition</dt>
+    <dd>The merge algorithm that combines two versions of a file by consulting a third — their common ancestor. Diffing each side against the base reveals <em>who changed what</em>; regions changed by only one side are taken automatically, identical changes are taken once, and regions changed differently by both sides become conflicts. It is line-based and content-agnostic: git merges text regions, never meanings.</dd>
+    <dt class="ex">Project example</dt>
+    <dd>Commit <code>24f142b</code> merged <code>origin/master</code> into <code>feature/issue-18-adc-reference-scaling</code>. For <code>PROGRESS.md</code> the three inputs were base <code>df5c43c</code> (no Session 3 at all), ours (Session 3 = the July ADC entry), theirs (Session 3 = the June visual-testing entry). Both sides inserted at the same point → one conflict hunk. Reproducible offline: <code>git merge-file -p --diff3 ours.md base.md theirs.md</code>.</dd>
+    <dt class="use">Usage</dt>
+    <dd>"Don't reason from the two branch tips alone — a three-way merge decides by each side's diff against the base, so look at what each branch <em>changed</em>, not how they differ."</dd>
+    <dt class="vs">Contrast with: two-way diff/merge</dt>
+    <dd>A two-way comparison sees only that the sides differ, not who diverged from what — every difference looks like a coin-flip. The base turns "these differ" into "ours added X, theirs added Y", which is what makes automatic merging (and the keep-both diagnosis) possible at all.</dd>
+  </dl>
+</div>
+
+<!-- ============================================================ -->
+<div class="term" id="merge-base">
+  <h2>Merge base</h2>
+  <div class="pron">git merge-base A B — the nearest common ancestor</div>
+  <dl>
+    <dt>Definition</dt>
+    <dd>The most recent commit reachable from both branches — the point where their histories diverged, and the "base" input of the three-way merge. Everything after it on either side is that side's contribution; the choice of base therefore decides what counts as "changed".</dd>
+    <dt class="ex">Project example</dt>
+    <dd><code>git merge-base 24f142b^1 24f142b^2</code> → <code>df5c43c</code>, whose log ends at Drill 1 — proving <em>neither</em> Session 3 existed before the fork and both were insertions. The later convergence merge <code>f2b6fbf</code> had a different base, <code>701853d</code>, where the ADC entry already existed as Session 3 — which is why theirs' rename of that heading registered as a change and merged silently with ours' appends.</dd>
+    <dt class="use">Usage</dt>
+    <dd>"Before merging, run <code>git log $(git merge-base HEAD origin/master)..origin/master -- PROGRESS.md</code> — everything it lists is what 'theirs' will bring to the table."</dd>
+    <dt class="vs">Contrast with: branch point you remember</dt>
+    <dd>After prior merges, the merge base moves: because <code>24f142b</code> merged master in, later merges use a much newer base than the original fork. The base is computed from the graph (lowest common ancestor), not from team folklore about "where we branched".</dd>
+  </dl>
+</div>
+
+<!-- ============================================================ -->
+<div class="term" id="conflict-hunk">
+  <h2>Conflict hunk</h2>
+  <div class="pron">&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt; ours ======= theirs &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;</div>
+  <dl>
+    <dt>Definition</dt>
+    <dd>The disputed region git writes into the working file when both sides changed the same region differently: ours' candidate above the <code>=======</code> separator, theirs' below, delimited by the <code>&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;</code>/<code>&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;</code> markers (plus the base text under <code>|||||||</code> in diff3 style). It is a question, not an error — resolving means replacing the entire hunk, markers included, with the final text.</dd>
+    <dt class="ex">Project example</dt>
+    <dd>The single hunk at 24f142b sat at PROGRESS.md's append anchor: ours' July "Session 3 (…issue #18)" block above the separator, theirs' June "Session 3 (2026-06-28, overnight)" block below, and — under diff3 — an <strong>empty base section</strong>, the fingerprint of both-sides-append. The resolution replaced the hunk with both entries, June first, July renumbered to Session 4.</dd>
+    <dt class="use">Usage</dt>
+    <dd>"Read the whole hunk before touching it — the dates and issue numbers inside it are the evidence for the keep-one vs keep-both diagnosis."</dd>
+    <dt class="vs">Contrast with: conflict markers</dt>
+    <dd>The markers are just the delimiters; the hunk is the content between them. Deleting the markers without merging the content (or missing one — a stray <code>=======</code>) "resolves" nothing: the post-merge sweep greps for anchored, length-exact marker lines precisely because git will happily commit leftovers.</dd>
+  </dl>
+</div>
+
+<!-- ============================================================ -->
+<div class="term" id="keep-both">
+  <h2>Keep-both resolution</h2>
+  <div class="pron">two events → two records</div>
+  <dl>
+    <dt>Definition</dt>
+    <dd>Resolving a conflict by including <em>both</em> sides' text — the correct outcome when the hunk holds two records of <strong>different events</strong> rather than two versions of one record. The kept entries are ordered by the file's own rule (not by git's ours-first layout), and any invariants that keeping both breaks (duplicate numbering) are repaired as part of the resolution.</dd>
+    <dt class="ex">Project example</dt>
+    <dd>24f142b kept master's June visual-testing entry <em>and</em> the branch's July ADC entry: different dates, different issues, and the merge itself brought lesson 0008's files into the tree — dropping either entry would make the log deny work the tree contains. June stayed Session 3; July was renumbered to Session 4.</dd>
+    <dt class="use">Usage</dt>
+    <dd>"Both sides appended and the diff3 base is empty — that's a keep-both: take both entries, chronological order, and renumber the later one."</dd>
+    <dt class="vs">Contrast with: <code>--ours</code> / <code>--theirs</code> (keep-one)</dt>
+    <dd>Keep-one is right when the sides are two versions of the <em>same</em> record (keep the revision or the superset). Applied to a both-sides-append log it silently deletes one branch's true history — the one-keystroke "resolution" that recreates exactly the data loss the conflict existed to prevent.</dd>
+  </dl>
+</div>
+
+<!-- ============================================================ -->
+<div class="term" id="semantic-invariant">
+  <h2>Semantic invariant</h2>
+  <div class="pron">the file's rules that git cannot see</div>
+  <dl>
+    <dt>Definition</dt>
+    <dd>A property a file must satisfy that lives <em>above</em> the line level — unique IDs, monotonic numbering, chronological order, exactly one tail section. Line-based tools can produce output that is textually consistent with both parents yet violates these rules: textual success is not semantic success.</dd>
+    <dt class="ex">Project example</dt>
+    <dd>PROGRESS.md's invariants: session numbers unique and monotonically increasing, entries chronological, one <code>## Suggested Next Lessons</code> tail. The f2b6fbf auto-merge (exit 0, zero markers) violated the first two — a duplicate <code>### Session 4</code> and an off-by-one tail — because ours' appended numbering was premised on an assumption theirs' renumbering had invalidated. Repair: renumber five headings, then assert <code>grep -oE '^### Session [0-9]+' | sort | uniq -d</code> prints nothing.</dd>
+    <dt class="use">Usage</dt>
+    <dd>"The merge was clean, but run the invariant checks anyway — clean only means git had no questions, not that the answers were right."</dd>
+    <dt class="vs">Contrast with: textual conflict</dt>
+    <dd>A textual conflict is loud — git stops and demands a decision. An invariant violation after a clean merge is silent — no message, no marker, wrong file. The loud failure is the safe one; the guard (pre-commit/CI assertion) exists for the silent one.</dd>
+  </dl>
+</div>
+
+<!-- ============================================================ -->
+<div class="term" id="append-anchor">
+  <h2>Append anchor</h2>
+  <div class="pron">where every writer inserts</div>
+  <dl>
+    <dt>Definition</dt>
+    <dd>The stable context lines between which an append-only file receives every new entry — end of the last record above, fixed tail below. Because all writers insert between the same context, any two branches that both appended have changed "the same region": append-only logs conflict at the anchor <strong>by construction</strong>, not by accident.</dd>
+    <dt class="ex">Project example</dt>
+    <dd>PROGRESS.md's anchor is the gap between the last session block and <code>## Suggested Next Lessons</code>. Master and the issue-18 branch each inserted a Session 3 there → the 24f142b conflict. Same shape: <code>GLOSSARY.md</code> (append-at-end table). The mitigation spectrum: per-entry files (as <code>lessons/</code> does), a union merge driver (dangerous), or accept-and-repair (this repo's choice).</dd>
+    <dt class="use">Usage</dt>
+    <dd>"Two overnight branches both logged sessions, so expect a conflict at the append anchor — that's the file working as designed, not someone's mistake."</dd>
+    <dt class="vs">Contrast with: ordinary code-edit collision</dt>
+    <dd>Source-code conflicts require the bad luck of touching the same lines; parallel work usually lands in different functions and merges cleanly. The anchor removes the luck: every append targets the same spot, so collision probability is ~100% per pair of appending branches. Also note the anchor only guards <em>pure appends</em> — once a side edits old headings too (the Session-3→4 rename), changes can slide past it silently (f2b6fbf).</dd>
+  </dl>
+</div>
+
+<!-- ============================================================ -->
+<div class="term" id="derived-file">
+  <h2>Derived file</h2>
+  <div class="pron">regenerate, don't merge</div>
+  <dl>
+    <dt>Definition</dt>
+    <dd>A file that is a deterministic projection of other sources — an index, lockfile, generated report, build output. It is not a source of truth, so merging it hunk-by-hunk answers the wrong question: the correct "resolution" of any conflict in it is to resolve the <em>sources</em>, then rerun the generator.</dd>
+    <dt class="ex">Project example</dt>
+    <dd><code>docs/learning/index.html</code> hit an add/add conflict at f2b6fbf: absent at base <code>701853d</code>, created independently on both sides (43 vs 54 lines, even disagreeing on doctype casing). Resolution: take neither; run <code>python3 docs/learning/.tools/build_index.py</code> against the merged directory. The regenerated 44-line file matches neither parent and indexes the union — more correct than both.</dd>
+    <dt class="use">Usage</dt>
+    <dd>"That's a generated index under conflict — don't pick a side, rebuild it after the lesson files land; that's also why tonight's session doesn't touch index.html."</dd>
+    <dt class="vs">Contrast with: source of truth</dt>
+    <dd>The test: "if I deleted this file, could one command recreate it?" Yes → derived; the command is the resolution. No → source of truth (PROGRESS.md, lesson HTML, Python code); merge it and resolve conflicts by diagnosis. Hand-merging derived output can, at absolute best, slowly imitate the generator.</dd>
+  </dl>
+</div>
+
+<!-- ============================================================ -->
+<div class="term" id="merge-tree-dry-run">
+  <h2>Merge-tree dry run</h2>
+  <div class="pron">git merge-tree — predict conflicts without touching anything</div>
+  <dl>
+    <dt>Definition</dt>
+    <dd>An in-memory merge that reports what <code>git merge</code> <em>would</em> do — conflicted paths and result tree — without moving <code>HEAD</code>, changing the working tree, or requiring it to be clean. Modern form: <code>git merge-tree --write-tree ours theirs</code> (git ≥ 2.38, nonzero exit on conflict); classic form: <code>git merge-tree base ours theirs</code>, which prints the would-be hunks.</dd>
+    <dt class="ex">Project example</dt>
+    <dd>Before converging tonight's stack, <code>git merge-tree --write-tree --no-messages HEAD origin/master</code> would have predicted the PROGRESS.md conflict at 24f142b in milliseconds — and its silence about PROGRESS.md before f2b6fbf is itself information: "this file will merge cleanly, so schedule the invariant check instead of a resolution."</dd>
+    <dt class="use">Usage</dt>
+    <dd>"Dry-run the merge first — if merge-tree lists PROGRESS.md, plan the keep-both resolution before you start, not in the middle of a half-merged tree."</dd>
+    <dt class="vs">Contrast with: <code>git merge --no-commit</code> / merge-and-abort</dt>
+    <dd>A real merge with <code>--no-commit</code> mutates the index and working tree and demands a clean state; <code>--abort</code> must then unwind it. merge-tree is pure computation on the object database — safe to run mid-work, in scripts, or in CI, and the natural first step of an overnight agent's convergence plan.</dd>
+  </dl>
+</div>
